MatTO began as a fork of FEniTop (Yingqi Jia, Chao Wang and Xiaojia
Shelly Zhang, "FEniTop: a simple FEniCSx implementation for 2D and 3D
topology optimization supporting parallel computing", Structural and
Multidisciplinary Optimization 67, 140 (2024),
doi:10.1007/s00158-024-03818-7), also GPL-3.0. What remains of FEniTop's
code, by file:

  src/matto/mma.py       The MMA solver and its subproblem solve are
                         FEniTop's, modified to take the communicator
                         as an argument and without the
                         optimality-criteria update. 250 of 263 code
                         lines are FEniTop's.

  src/matto/utility.py   Communicator (the serial/parallel dof map used
                         to gather fields for the saved arrays) and
                         compare_matrices are FEniTop's. 54 of 232 code
                         lines.

  src/matto/design.py    The Helmholtz filter assembly in HelmholtzKernel
                         (the Kf and T forms, their matrices and solver
                         setup) and the smoothed-Heaviside projection
                         formula in HeavisideProjection are FEniTop's
                         DensityFilter and Heaviside, rewritten behind
                         the Operator interface. 11 of 572 code lines
                         are verbatim.

src/matto/driver.py, state.py and sensitivity.py began as modified
copies of FEniTop's topopt.py, fem.py and sensitivity.py and were
rewritten for a nonlinear state problem, several design fields and
several load cases; no FEniTop code remains in them. The sensitivity
follows the same approach of differentiating the UFL forms with respect
to the physical field and solving an adjoint, generalized to the
nonlinear residual. The material models, the Material contract, the
tests and the examples are MatTO's.

The FEniTop copyright notice is kept on the three files that contain
FEniTop code. The git history records the lineage of the rest.
